Installing cadaver
==================

README.md has the full instructions, including how to install the
toolchain. This is the short form.

There is no `configure' script in a fresh clone: it is generated.
Nothing has to be fetched separately, though -- neon is checked into the
tree rather than being a submodule.


Windows, with autotools
-----------------------

In an MSYS2 UCRT64 shell:

    ./autogen.sh
    ./configure --with-ssl=openssl
    make

That leaves cadaver.exe at the top of the tree. `make install' puts it
and the manual page under $prefix, which defaults to /ucrt64; use
`./configure --prefix=...' to choose another.


Windows, without autotools
--------------------------

The answers configure works out are checked in at win32/config.h, so
the build needs only GNU make and a MinGW-w64 compiler:

    make -f Makefile.w32

and, for an executable that runs on a machine with no MSYS2 installed:

    make -f Makefile.w32 STATIC=1


What has to be installed first
------------------------------

A MinGW-w64 compiler, and OpenSSL, expat, readline and iconv for it.
On MSYS2:

    pacman -S --needed autoconf automake libtool m4 make pkgconf \
        mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-gcc mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-openssl \
        mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-expat mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-readline \
        mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-libiconv mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-pkgconf

The autotools packages are only needed for the first build path.


Configure options
-----------------

    --with-ssl=openssl     use OpenSSL for TLS (the only choice tested
                           here; --with-ssl=gnutls also exists)
    --enable-warnings      build with the warning set CI gates on
    --disable-readline     build without line editing, history and
                           tab completion
    --without-iconv        build without character-set conversion, which
                           then requires a UTF-8 terminal
    --disable-netrc        build without .netrc support
    --disable-debugging    build without the `set debug' tracing

`./configure --help' lists the rest, including the standard directory
options.


Checking the build
------------------

    make check

runs the whole battery: the checks that need no server, the unit tests
for the globbing, and the scripted sessions against two local WebDAV
servers. README.md explains what each needs.
